WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:08.510
SIERRA: The global arms trade doesn’t enjoy
a particularly sunny reputation.

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:10.780
And honestly, fair.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:15.879
Groups like Amnesty International have condemned
the industry for its lack of oversight, noting

00:00:15.879 --> 00:00:18.390
the deadly cost paid by civilians.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:23.260
The United States is the biggest arms dealer
on earth.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:32.259
From 2017 to 2021 it sold weapons to over
100 nations, and in 2020 alone, American companies

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:36.570
made $111 billion from foreign military sales.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:41.421
While this may sound like a lot, profits are
actually only a part of why the U.S. does

00:00:41.421 --> 00:00:42.421
it.

00:00:42.421 --> 00:00:47.420
Behind the scenes, arms sales are a common
foreign policy tool, giving the U.S. leverage

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:53.590
over the countries it sells to, and, according
to some, helping it to shape behavior, conflicts,

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:56.190
and security all over the world.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.850
Take our involvement in the Ukraine conflict.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:03.130
By sending billions of dollars in weapons,
along with intelligence and training, the

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:08.280
U.S. was able to provide crucial support to
Ukraine as it pushed back against invading

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.030
Russian forces.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:13.860
All without risking a single U.S. soldier
on the battlefield.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.950
But it isn’t always so clear-cut.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:21.391
In Yemen, Saudi Arabia has used American weapons
to commit human rights abuses as part of a

00:01:21.391 --> 00:01:24.100
war the United States has opposed.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:29.740
In the Afghanistan war, American soldiers
faced enemies using weapons first provided

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:33.850
by the U.S. to anti-Soviet mujahideen decades
before.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:37.799
This is a complex, and messy business.

00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:41.799
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It
Matters.

00:01:41.799 --> 00:01:46.969
Today, the reality behind U.S. dominance in
the global arms trade.

00:01:46.969 --> 00:01:56.789
David D. Eisenhower: Good evening my fellow
Americans, until the latest of our world conflicts,

00:01:56.789 --> 00:02:01.009
the United States had no armaments industry.

00:02:01.009 --> 00:02:05.650
But we can no longer risk emergency improvisation
of national defense.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:10.200
We have been compelled to create a permanent
armaments industry of vast proportions.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.360
Joseph Biden Jr.: Last week I signed an 800
million dollar package of security assistance

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.360
to Ukraine.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:20.930
Today, I’m announcing another 800 million
dollars to further augment Ukraine’s ability

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:21.930
to fight in the East.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.720
Donald J. Trump: If we don’t sell it to
‘em, they’ll say ‘well, thank you very

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.540
much, we’ll buy it from Russia’ or ‘thank
you very much, we’ll buy it from China.’

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.570
Barack Obama: I can also announce that the
United States is fully lifting the ban on

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:39.550
the sale of military equipment to Vietnam
that has been in place for some 50 years.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:46.099
Gabrielle SIERRA: If I'm someone who comes
in knowing nothing about arms trade, which

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:50.830
I have a feeling many of us might be in that
camp, do you think you can give me just a

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:53.270
sort of 101 rundown?

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:55.100
What are the key things that I should know?

00:02:55.100 --> 00:03:00.319
Rachel STOHL: We don't often think about the
arms trade when we're reading news stories

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:06.420
or hearing about current events in the news,
but it's sort of front and center in the tools

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.590
that are used by Washington in its foreign
policy arsenal.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.030
SIERRA: This is Rachel Stohl.

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:17.470
She is the vice president of research programs
at the Stimson Center, where she also directs

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.970
their conventional defense program.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:25.760
STOHL: The global arms trade is an integral
part of governments and their foreign policy

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.680
and defense policies around the world.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:33.700
The United States commands by far the largest
share of the arms market.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:39.220
It's more than 40% of the global arms trade
just in the last year alone, and that's next

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.909
to twice the size of its closest global competitor.

00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:47.390
So really they're unrivaled and unparalleled
in their domination of the arms market.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:52.680
Bill HARTUNG: There's a handful of supplier
nations that control the bulk of the trade

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:57.540
in major weapons - tanks, aircraft, helicopters,
missiles, et cetera.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.299
SIERRA: This is Bill Hartung.

00:03:59.299 --> 00:04:05.190
He is a senior research fellow at the Quincy
Institute for Responsible Statecraft, he focuses

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:10.540
on among other topics, global arms trade,
military strategy and nuclear policy.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.189
HARTUNG: And the United States is at the top
of the list.

00:04:13.189 --> 00:04:14.800
Russia is about half of that.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.590
China's about one seventh.

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:17.690
France is a player.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:20.150
UK and Germany are in the mix.

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:23.330
But basically the top five control three quarters
of the trade.

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:28.331
So when you've got this big military complex,
there's an incentive to export because it

00:04:28.331 --> 00:04:31.830
makes bigger production lines cheaper per
weapon.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:35.840
It's very profitable for companies to sell
overseas when the government has already paid

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.490
for them to develop the weapons.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:45.660
SIERRA: One reason U.S. foreign arms sales
lead the pack is that the U.S. spends an extraordinary

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:48.150
amount on its military overall.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:53.889
Defense spending hit $801 billion in 2021
alone.

00:04:53.889 --> 00:04:59.400
Compare that to the next highest spender,
China, at $293 billion.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:06.229
The US is also home to 4 of the top 5 private
arms manufacturers in the world.

00:05:06.229 --> 00:05:11.990
Companies like Lockheed Martin, Boeing, Northrup
Grumman, and Raytheon Technologies are synonymous

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:14.030
with the business of war.

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:19.250
It's a bit like silicon valley - where American
companies tower over a global industry.

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:23.139
So how did the U.S. get here?

00:05:23.139 --> 00:05:29.310
Well, after WWII, the U.S. arms industry grew
rapidly throughout the Cold War.

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:34.000
The United States was spooked about Soviet
expansion and increased its military budget

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:35.240
as a result.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:40.669
Preferring proxy conflicts to outright war,
the U.S. and Russia increasingly shipped weapons

00:05:40.669 --> 00:05:43.030
to smaller nations.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:50.550
Budgets had grown so much that by 1960, President
Eisenhower warned of a booming "military industrial

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:54.990
complex", leading to decades of debate about
how much the arms trade, and private arms

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:57.360
industry, fuels conflict.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:03.340
SIERRA: How does the arms industry compare
to other large scale industries, like agriculture

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:04.340
or oil?

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:05.340
STOHL: Sure.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:11.169
So if we compare the arms trade to other industries,
the international defense trade remains relatively

00:06:11.169 --> 00:06:13.020
small, but that's in terms of dollar value.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:18.110
So if you look at the Stockholm International
Peace Research Institute or SIPRI, they put

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:22.510
the value of the arms trade around $212 billion
in 2019.

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:28.590
Now, if you compared that to the global trade
in agriculture, that was approximately $1.4

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:30.099
trillion dollars in 2020.

00:06:30.099 --> 00:06:34.199
SIERRA: Okay, because yeah, that sounded like
a big number to me, but definitely not in

00:06:34.199 --> 00:06:35.199
comparison.

00:06:35.199 --> 00:06:36.199
Okay.

00:06:36.199 --> 00:06:37.550
STOHL: So that gives you sort of a sense of
the enterprise.

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:42.340
But I don't want people to be too bogged down
in sort of the numbers and the dollar value

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:48.830
because when you're thinking about the impact
of the global arms trade, it's really vast,

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:49.830
right?

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:54.910
It involves or impacts nearly every country
on the planet, whether they're arms exporters,

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:59.650
arms importers, maybe they're a transit or
a trans shipment country, maybe they feel

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:04.940
the effects of the arms trade in terms of,
like, refugee flows, or maybe they're involved

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:07.039
in conflict or neighboring conflict.

00:07:07.039 --> 00:07:09.530
So there are many different downstream consequences.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:15.379
Now, obviously, we don't intend for innocent
civilians to be killed by the global arms

00:07:15.379 --> 00:07:16.379
trade, but it happens.

00:07:16.379 --> 00:07:21.020
And so we have to think about how the arms
trade fits into larger policy concerns.

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:22.020
SIERRA: Right?

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:26.870
These are weapons or tools that can lead to
death and destruction.

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:30.550
STOHL: Deadly consequences all across the
board.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:38.789
SIERRA: Is there a spectrum of relationships
from a full on adversary like Iran, to whom

00:07:38.789 --> 00:07:45.440
we would never sell weapons to an ally like
Ukraine, to whom we provide weapons for free,

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:51.199
to a NATO ally like the United Kingdom, who
we are committed to defend, you know, unconditionally

00:07:51.199 --> 00:07:52.509
from attack?

00:07:52.509 --> 00:07:55.470
Where do arms deals fall on such a spectrum?

00:07:55.470 --> 00:08:01.360
HARTUNG: The idea of a spectrum is a good
one because as mentioned, countries like Iran

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.159
or Syria, the United States is never going
to sell them weapons unless there's a dramatic

00:08:06.159 --> 00:08:08.789
political earthquake of some sort.

00:08:08.789 --> 00:08:13.800
In Ukraine, it's of the moment, the weapons
are vital for it to defend itself.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:19.080
A country like the UK or another NATO ally
there is a commitment to defend them and therefore,

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:21.550
sales to those countries are relatively uncontroversial.

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:24.710
Although they're an advanced economy, so they
pay for the weapons, they don't get them as

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:25.860
aid.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:29.759
And then there's a big group in the middle,
some African allies, some Middle Eastern allies

00:08:29.759 --> 00:08:34.150
and others where there's human rights issues,
maybe there's been a military coup or there's

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:35.390
active conflicts.

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:38.669
So in all of those cases, there's judgment
calls to be made.

00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:41.070
There's no automatic decision to make the
sale.

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:42.360
There may be conditions.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:46.370
You may withhold weapons at a certain point
if conflict is heating up and you don't want

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:47.450
to stoke it.

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:52.300
So it ranges from countries that the sales
are uncontroversial, in some cases urgent,

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:56.529
in some cases for future deterrence, then
you've got this problematic group, and then

00:08:56.529 --> 00:09:01.290
you've got a group that's completely beyond
the pale because they're long standing adversaries.

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:08.430
SIERRA: To get just like a little more granular,
step by step, how do these agreements start,

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:09.600
who is approving them?

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:10.899
How long does it take?

00:09:10.899 --> 00:09:11.899
How does it go?

00:09:11.899 --> 00:09:16.930
STOHL: I think at the most basic level, what
we can say is the U.S. engages and arms transfers

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:18.970
under several headings.

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:24.550
So the first is government to government sales,
also known as foreign military sales in which

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:28.570
the U.S. government sells directly to another
government.

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:34.200
And those are overseen by the Department of
Defense with inter agency feedback about those

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:35.680
particular sales.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:40.630
SIERRA: Direct foreign military sales happen
all the time and it’s the one we hear about

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:41.630
most.

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:47.699
This October, for example, the US sold missiles
to Japan, to the tune of $450 million.

00:09:47.699 --> 00:09:53.510
These types of decisions, and arms sales policy
in general, are made at the executive level.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:56.080
Presidents have significant leeway to shape
them.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:01.020
But with direct foreign military sales there
is also a complicated review process.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:06.769
It includes the embassy, the combatant commander
of a particular region, the Defense Security

00:10:06.769 --> 00:10:12.130
Cooperation Agency, Secretaries of State,
Defense, Commerce, and Trade.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:15.330
And in the end, Congress also has to approve.

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:18.950
It can take a while but it is a reliable process.

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:26.570
STOHL: The second one is called direct commercial
sales, and that's where a U.S. vendor or company

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:32.480
engages directly with a foreign client on
a sale.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:37.829
That client could be a government, it could
be a military, it could be a police force,

00:10:37.829 --> 00:10:41.470
it could be any number of private entities.

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:48.370
That is licensed by the Department of State,
but that deal is negotiated by the industry

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:49.480
and the client.

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:51.600
So it's a very different sort of system.

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:56.320
Now, there's still an inter agency review
process of those sales as well to be able

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:58.980
to get a license from the State Department.

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:05.389
SIERRA: Lockheed Martin, which sits at the
top of commercial arms sales, sold over $67

00:11:05.389 --> 00:11:07.589
billion dollars worth of weapons in 2021.

00:11:07.589 --> 00:11:14.279
In fact, they were the sole contractor for
the largest foreign deal of the year - a $6.9

00:11:14.279 --> 00:11:19.920
billion dollar heavy hitter that offered four
combat ships and naval equipment to Greece.

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:24.899
Direct commercial sales still need an export
license and congressional approval, but they

00:11:24.899 --> 00:11:27.470
are popular due to the shorter review time.

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:33.330
STOHL: And then the final sort of category,
I'm just going to call U.S. security assistance,

00:11:33.330 --> 00:11:40.020
where the U.S. provides defense articles or
services through aid that's funded by the

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:43.390
U.S. taxpayer, but that goes to a foreign
government.

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:46.660
So we hear a lot about that in the context
of Ukraine, for example.

00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:52.600
When we hear about drawdowns from U.S. stockpiles,
there are things called excess defense articles.

00:11:52.600 --> 00:11:58.010
There's many different programs that cover
this range of security assistance, which is

00:11:58.010 --> 00:12:03.680
really an aid based program versus a, I'm
paying you to purchase a particular item.

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:08.399
SIERRA: Regardless of which type of sale the
U.S. engages in, there’s a lot going on

00:12:08.399 --> 00:12:11.420
besides profits for arms manufacturers.

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:15.420
The sales provide an economic benefit for
the U.S. military too.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:19.970
Christa ALMONTE: Taxpayers don't pay for weapons
that go abroad, but they do pay for weapons

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:21.670
that come to our own forces.

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:24.870
SIERRA: This is Captain Christa Almonte.

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:27.310
She is a foreign area officer for the U.S.
Navy.

00:12:27.310 --> 00:12:32.360
She is also a visiting fellow here at the
Council, focusing on defense, security and

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:33.360
intelligence.

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:39.940
ALMONTE: If we, say, for example, we put an
order in for 10 helicopters with a U.S. industry.

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:44.649
Five of those are going to go overseas, but
five are going to go to our armed forces,

00:12:44.649 --> 00:12:49.000
and we get those cheaper because we basically
bought them by the dozen instead of just the

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:50.810
six that we needed.

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:54.250
That's an actual benefit to us that taxpayers
can feel.

00:12:54.250 --> 00:12:58.519
And remember, foreign military sales is, by
no means, limited to arms.

00:12:58.519 --> 00:13:01.240
It can be desalinization kits.

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:03.290
It can be flashlights.

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:05.300
It can be night vision goggles.

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:06.300
It can be Jeeps.

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:07.730
It can be just about anything.

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:14.400
SIERRA: Amazing that sales work the same way,
whether you're buying an avocado or a weapon,

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:16.899
that if you buy in bulk you get a good deal.

00:13:16.899 --> 00:13:17.899
ALMONTE: Exactly.

00:13:17.899 --> 00:13:19.970
ALMONTE: But there's also another side to
it.

00:13:19.970 --> 00:13:27.510
Every time a company makes a helicopter or
a Jeep for another country, they do have that

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:29.130
follow-on technical support.

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:34.029
That means it keeps the line open - production
line open over the years.

00:13:34.029 --> 00:13:38.360
The United States may have that Jeep, but
we don't need the maintenance on it as much.

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:41.459
We do better upkeep, we already have more
spare parts.

00:13:41.459 --> 00:13:45.029
And so we would not, ourselves, be keeping
the production line open.

00:13:45.029 --> 00:13:49.700
But because we are still selling it to other
countries, it does keep it open.

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:52.420
It keeps that manufacturer in business.

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:57.160
Whether it's a mom-and-pop manufacturer or
it's a Lockheed Martin and a Boeing of America,

00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:58.779
it keeps it open.

00:13:58.779 --> 00:14:03.320
That's important because 10 years, 20 years
from now, we may need those spare parts and

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:08.019
if we haven't been selling, then there's been
no reason to keep the production line open.

00:14:08.019 --> 00:14:12.780
And frankly, we can't find spare parts to
our own equipment and companies will not necessarily

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:14.899
open the production line back up for us.

00:14:14.899 --> 00:14:18.699
They are selling other things and they're
not going to reopen when it's not beneficial

00:14:18.699 --> 00:14:20.500
to their own bottom line as well.

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:25.220
SIERRA: Because the U.S. relies on private
industry to arm its large military, it needs

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:29.440
those companies to thrive in the periods between
U.S. purchases.

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:33.709
The argument goes that foreign sales allow
companies to keep operating - and developing

00:14:33.709 --> 00:14:38.510
new weapons- so that they’re ready the next
time the U.S. needs something new.

00:14:38.510 --> 00:14:43.550
SIERRA: Is there also a benefit that our allies
have the same technology as us?

00:14:43.550 --> 00:14:44.910
You know, is it more secure?

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:46.089
ALMONTE: It is.

00:14:46.089 --> 00:14:50.050
So there's a few different reasons why technology
from the United States, it's much better for

00:14:50.050 --> 00:14:52.130
us to operate with our own technology.

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:55.570
Number one we understand it, it can talk to
itself.

00:14:55.570 --> 00:15:00.029
If someone gets a piece of equipment from,
let's just say a European country or from

00:15:00.029 --> 00:15:04.699
China, they don't necessarily interoperate
and we can literally sail right past each

00:15:04.699 --> 00:15:09.870
other, or fly right past each other, and not
be able to have secure communications.

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:14.019
That's not helpful if you're trying to do
an operation or an exercise and you don't

00:15:14.019 --> 00:15:17.860
want people to know the details of it, other
than those you're operating with.

00:15:17.860 --> 00:15:21.740
SIERRA: OK, so we understand how foreign arms
sales happen.

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:24.350
But what about why?

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:28.740
Beyond the economic benefits, why does the
U.S. sell weapons to countries all over the

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:29.740
world?

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:34.910
ALMONTE: The simple answer is, U.S. weapon
sales and security assistance programs build

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:40.120
security partnerships among partner nations,
among allies, and with those nations that

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:45.540
are ready and willing but not quite able in
some aspect to advance national security objectives

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:51.589
that are similar to our own here in the U.S.
When we enable partners, we essentially multiply

00:15:51.589 --> 00:15:59.230
the forces that we have to answer a call for
a defense, or security, or humanitarian assistance.

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:01.829
We give them the ability to do for themselves.

00:16:01.829 --> 00:16:06.230
So we don't have to send our own forces around
the globe quite so often.

00:16:06.230 --> 00:16:07.759
And it is a foreign policy tool.

00:16:07.759 --> 00:16:11.899
You can see in the news today, President Biden
is again reviewing sales agreements already

00:16:11.899 --> 00:16:18.220
signed by his predecessors with Saudi Arabia
as a result of the OPEC agreement to decrease

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:22.930
the oil production, and that was, of course,
largely Saudi Arabia and Russia.

00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:28.890
So it is a tool that the president has at
his disposal to sway governments one way or

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:29.890
the other.

00:16:29.890 --> 00:16:33.370
If a country does something that we weren't
expecting them to do when we agreed to the

00:16:33.370 --> 00:16:37.019
contract, then we certainly have the ability
to put that on hold.

00:16:37.019 --> 00:16:42.790
STOHL: Arms transfers are seen by the policy
establishment as contributing to several overarching

00:16:42.790 --> 00:16:45.290
national security and foreign policy aims.

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:50.100
And I think the first one is really very obvious
and the most concrete, easiest for us to wrap

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:55.410
our head around, which is we're contributing
to the military capability and capacities

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:56.480
of foreign partners.

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:59.930
So they may be addressing shared security
threats.

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:02.800
Maybe we want to improve military interoperability.

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:07.350
Maybe we want to provide them weapons for
their own self-defense.

00:17:07.350 --> 00:17:10.179
It's a very straightforward reason.

00:17:10.179 --> 00:17:16.140
But the results are particularly uneven, particularly
over the last few decades.

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:21.829
We also may engage, and when I say we, I mean
the United States, may often engage in arms

00:17:21.829 --> 00:17:27.309
transfers as a means to develop access and
influence with a foreign partner.

00:17:27.309 --> 00:17:33.720
So that would include things like foreign
basing or over flight rights, or allow the

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:40.370
U.S. military to conduct activities in a foreign
country and to really build a defense relationship.

00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:46.210
Now, I should add that influence is really,
really difficult to quantify.

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:50.990
Much of the U.S. arms trade sort of rests
on the assumption that consistent security

00:17:50.990 --> 00:17:55.590
cooperation with a foreign partner helps build
U.S. influence.

00:17:55.590 --> 00:18:00.390
I think in reality the impact is really difficult
to measure.

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:06.720
And we can look at lots of anecdotal episodes
suggesting that the efficacy of security cooperation

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:11.820
in shaping the behavior of a foreign partner
to act in a way that the U.S. wants it to

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:14.220
act is really questionable.

00:18:14.220 --> 00:18:21.600
SIERRA: Are there good examples from history
when the U.S. has regretted providing or selling

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:22.600
arms?

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:27.831
HARTUNG: If you look at when the Soviet Union
invaded Afghanistan at the end of the '70s,

00:18:27.831 --> 00:18:32.860
the US strategy was to arm the Mujahideen
- some of the extremist groups that were helping

00:18:32.860 --> 00:18:34.419
drive the Soviets back.

00:18:34.419 --> 00:18:39.520
And some of those players went on to become
part of the base of al-Qaeda.

00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:45.000
Osama bin Laden was part of the supply chain
for that pushback against the Soviets.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:49.490
So you had this blow back or what I call the
boomerang effect, where you sell arms for

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:53.590
one reason and they come back to be used against
you in another context.

00:18:53.590 --> 00:18:57.370
Hillary Clinton: We were just so happy to
see the Soviet Union fall and we thought ‘okay

00:18:57.370 --> 00:18:58.370
fine, we’re okay now.

00:18:58.370 --> 00:19:00.210
Things are going to be so much better.’

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:05.820
Now, you look back, the people we’re fighting
today, we were supporting in the fight against

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:06.920
the Soviets.

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:12.821
HARTUNG: In Iraq, because of ISIS, and even
before that because of corruption, many of

00:19:12.821 --> 00:19:18.299
the US weapons ended up in the hands of anti
US forces that had been supplied to the Iraqi

00:19:18.299 --> 00:19:19.299
government.

00:19:19.299 --> 00:19:20.299
Some were lost in Yemen.

00:19:20.299 --> 00:19:21.950
There's various other places where this has
happened.

00:19:21.950 --> 00:19:26.960
So it's like once you sell these arms, you
... And they will last for decades.

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:29.179
They're usable for long periods of time.

00:19:29.179 --> 00:19:31.669
Politics will change, the chaos of war.

00:19:31.669 --> 00:19:35.970
For various reasons, they can end up on the
wrong side relative to US interests and so

00:19:35.970 --> 00:19:38.630
that's a big concern.

00:19:38.630 --> 00:19:45.360
STOHL: I think our security cooperation history
is sort of replete with examples of transfers

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:48.450
that in retrospect, were deeply regrettable.

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:53.960
I mean, you can go all the way back to the
Arms For Hostages deal that eventually became

00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:55.960
known as the Iran Contra Affair.

00:19:55.960 --> 00:20:02.120
You can think about security forces that would
use them in violation of human rights or international

00:20:02.120 --> 00:20:03.600
humanitarian law.

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:08.771
But we can go back to many, many other cases
throughout history where we know U.S. arms

00:20:08.771 --> 00:20:13.120
have been used in contravention of sort of
U.S. interests and U.S. values.

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:14.120
And that's sort of the problem.

00:20:14.120 --> 00:20:19.890
When your values don't align with the recipient
country and you can't influence their behavior,

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:21.790
often you see that mismatch.

00:20:21.790 --> 00:20:26.970
SIERRA: Many agree with Rachel that the U.S.
should consider selling fewer weapons to countries

00:20:26.970 --> 00:20:31.800
that don’t share U.S. values: particularly
authoritarian countries.

00:20:31.800 --> 00:20:36.630
But in some administrations, officials saw
that as yielding the field and competitive

00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:40.000
balance to arms rivals like Russia and China.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:43.169
STOHL: I don't want to make the case that
all arm sales are bad.

00:20:43.169 --> 00:20:44.610
I certainly don't believe that.

00:20:44.610 --> 00:20:49.940
I think there are cases where we've seen necessary
or positive outcomes.

00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:55.990
I think Ukraine right now is a very good example
of where effective U.S. security cooperation

00:20:55.990 --> 00:21:00.200
in the short term, and I want to say we don't
know what's going to happen with these weapons

00:21:00.200 --> 00:21:05.450
in the long term, but for right now, we understand
that short term need to provide those weapons.

00:21:05.450 --> 00:21:06.670
There are lots of others.

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:11.960
We can go back to U.S. military cooperation
with Europe after World War II and sort of

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:15.559
how our Western allies have rebuilt their
militaries.

00:21:15.559 --> 00:21:21.409
And we don't generally have sort of negative
views of arms transfers to our NATO allies.

00:21:21.409 --> 00:21:23.440
Those are positive relationships.

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:29.970
I think the big sort of overall problem is
when we use security cooperation as a bandaid

00:21:29.970 --> 00:21:39.539
for a short term crisis, but we don't consider
long term strategic thinking about desired

00:21:39.539 --> 00:21:43.830
outcome and potential impacts, that's where
we have that disconnect.

00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:49.390
SIERRA: For years, this type of thinking was
not very common in the U.S. government - but

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:52.480
there is evidence that some of that might
be changing.

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:59.020
STOHL: In the last decade, we've really seen
the political salience of arms transfers and

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:05.090
responsible security cooperation grow substantially,
and particularly among lawmakers.

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:10.610
I mean, I've had more conversations about
the arms trade in the last decade than probably

00:22:10.610 --> 00:22:12.669
the 15, 20 years before.

00:22:12.669 --> 00:22:18.111
People just are aware whether we're talking
about Saudi Arabia and the war in Yemen, whether

00:22:18.111 --> 00:22:22.669
we're talking about Ukraine, Afghanistan,
Iraq, it's just a more politically salient

00:22:22.669 --> 00:22:23.669
topic.

00:22:23.669 --> 00:22:31.960
And we're seeing more and more members of
Congress raise concerns about particular arms

00:22:31.960 --> 00:22:32.960
transfers.

00:22:32.960 --> 00:22:38.340
Rand Paul: Saudi Arabia’s air and naval
blockade of Yemen has led to thousands and

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:42.010
thousands of deaths in Yemen from lack of
food and medicine.

00:22:42.010 --> 00:22:48.640
The U.S. should end all arms sales to the
Saudis until they end their blockade of Yemen.

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:52.840
Elizabeth Warren: We need to ask ourselves
if the benefits of this relationship with

00:22:52.840 --> 00:22:56.990
Saudi Arabia is worth the costs if this kind
of behavior continues.

00:22:56.990 --> 00:23:02.840
Chris Murphy: Every single one of those bombs
is stamped with made in the USA, and Yemenis

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:03.840
know this.

00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:08.299
They don’t see this as a Saudi bombing campaign,
they see this as an American bombing campaign.

00:23:08.299 --> 00:23:15.520
STOHL: And we've even seen legislation around
arms transfers in order to strengthen the

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:21.200
guardrails that are meant to avert risky or
damaging U.S. arms transfers.

00:23:21.200 --> 00:23:27.890
And I think there was a joint resolution of
disapproval passed in 2019, and then lots

00:23:27.890 --> 00:23:32.549
of bills just in the last three years since
that were aimed at limiting U.S. military

00:23:32.549 --> 00:23:36.210
cooperation with Saudi Arabia and other human
rights abusers.

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:40.309
They did not have a veto proof majority, which
is why they did not pass.

00:23:40.309 --> 00:23:45.549
But it was really notable that for the first
time you had bipartisan support to actually

00:23:45.549 --> 00:23:47.950
disapprove of an arms transfer.

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:53.070
There's lots of things Congress can do to
flip the script a little bit and have to approve

00:23:53.070 --> 00:23:57.660
arms transfers rather than only being allowed
to vote to disapprove them.

00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:03.130
But it's a really interesting time, I think,
for Congress to re-engage in this conversation.

00:24:03.130 --> 00:24:05.430
SIERRA: Why are you seeing this now?

00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:10.680
STOHL: I think that we really have seen this
in the aftermath of what's happened with Saudi

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:17.679
Arabia, both with their perpetuation of the
war in Yemen with the killing of Jamal Khashoggi.

00:24:17.679 --> 00:24:23.700
I think there's just more light being shed
on who our partners are and the behaviors

00:24:23.700 --> 00:24:25.370
that they undertake.

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:33.100
And I think also there's been a question of
how has the U.S. been able to influence the

00:24:33.100 --> 00:24:37.520
behavior of Saudi Arabia through its arms
transfer relationship?

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:42.669
And I think the most recent OPEC production
cut is a really great example because it's

00:24:42.669 --> 00:24:50.159
a pretty fairly blunt rejection of the longstanding
oil for guns bargain that has defined the

00:24:50.159 --> 00:24:52.000
U.S. Saudi relationship.

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:57.370
And so I think there's just more attention
being paid to are we actually able to influence

00:24:57.370 --> 00:24:59.679
the behavior of the recipients of these arms?

00:24:59.679 --> 00:25:02.760
And the answer is overwhelmingly time and
time again, no.

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:06.380
And so I think people are questioning on that
sort of ethical and moral ground.

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:12.280
I mean, when you see a school bus of children
killed by a U.S. weapon that was never intended

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:17.179
to target civilians, you have to ask yourself
questions like is this really where we want

00:25:17.179 --> 00:25:18.179
to be involved?

00:25:18.179 --> 00:25:21.870
And I think members of Congress are finally
asking those questions.

00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:27.460
SIERRA: Oversight before a sale is one thing,
but these sales also create ongoing partnerships,

00:25:27.460 --> 00:25:30.669
not least because of the need for parts and
maintenance.

00:25:30.669 --> 00:25:34.580
As such, the U.S. often conducts oversight
after a sale.

00:25:34.580 --> 00:25:39.000
But experts do disagree on how effective this
oversight ultimately is.

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:42.820
ALMONTE: Depending upon the case, the ongoing
relationship can last years.

00:25:42.820 --> 00:25:46.840
It can last a lot longer than the initial
case, for example.

00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:52.309
When I was the Security Assistance Officer
in Bahrain, one of my duties was what we call,

00:25:52.309 --> 00:25:53.490
end-use monitoring.

00:25:53.490 --> 00:25:58.529
So, an item is delivered to a country and
they have it in use.

00:25:58.529 --> 00:26:03.380
They have it in their garrison, they're using
it in defense of whatever it is, let's say

00:26:03.380 --> 00:26:05.240
night vision goggles, for example.

00:26:05.240 --> 00:26:11.640
I then had to go and account for all of these
items to make sure the country had not transferred

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:13.289
them anywhere else.

00:26:13.289 --> 00:26:18.870
I literally had to go find the bases where
I knew they were keeping the material and

00:26:18.870 --> 00:26:24.120
I had a list with the serial numbers and the
amounts and quantities and they would lay

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:30.789
out 50, or 100, or 150 of these items and
I would check off one serial number at a time.

00:26:30.789 --> 00:26:34.909
I would take occasionally another American
with me from the embassy to do it because

00:26:34.909 --> 00:26:37.140
it really is a comprehensive assessment.

00:26:37.140 --> 00:26:42.799
I couldn't have one of the host nation men
do it because it's about American eyes on

00:26:42.799 --> 00:26:47.160
these items to make sure they are where they
are said they are, and they're being used

00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:48.669
for what they promised they would be used
for.

00:26:48.669 --> 00:26:49.669
SIERRA: Wow.

00:26:49.669 --> 00:26:50.669
And that's an ongoing process.

00:26:50.669 --> 00:26:51.980
ALMONTE: It's absolutely ongoing.

00:26:51.980 --> 00:26:56.460
It's a cyclical process and depending upon
the equipment it can happen every year.

00:26:56.460 --> 00:27:00.660
STOHL: So some systems are easier to track
than others.

00:27:00.660 --> 00:27:04.170
Obviously we think about using small arms
as an example.

00:27:04.170 --> 00:27:09.500
There's a serial number on that gun, and you
can track it from point A to point B to point

00:27:09.500 --> 00:27:16.649
C. Other systems also have identifying numbers
and characteristics that allow you to track

00:27:16.649 --> 00:27:21.010
them and our end use monitoring very much
looks like, "Okay, does this serial number

00:27:21.010 --> 00:27:26.350
or this box match what it says on this piece
of paper that we actually provided?

00:27:26.350 --> 00:27:29.380
Is it where we said it would be?"

00:27:29.380 --> 00:27:30.520
That does happen.

00:27:30.520 --> 00:27:34.740
What becomes harder is who is utilizing that
system.

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:41.360
We may say, this can go to this unit or for
this purpose, but once it gets across the

00:27:41.360 --> 00:27:45.880
water or the land, it's difficult to keep
track of what is where.

00:27:45.880 --> 00:27:48.440
And we've seen particularly in conflict zones.

00:27:48.440 --> 00:27:53.300
So you think Afghanistan, Iraq, or even Ukraine
at the moment, on just recent examples, it's

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:59.280
very difficult to say in the moment, this
is where all of those systems are that we

00:27:59.280 --> 00:28:00.320
have provided.

00:28:00.320 --> 00:28:03.560
Some systems are easier to do that with than
others.

00:28:03.560 --> 00:28:10.080
It's very hard to quickly and quietly move
a tank or an aircraft, but it's a lot easier

00:28:10.080 --> 00:28:12.300
when you're talking about small arms and light
weapons.

00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:14.870
So your risks are very different as well.

00:28:14.870 --> 00:28:18.990
SIERRA: I was going to ask, is this a structural
issue, like we just don't have the amount

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:24.121
of people that we need and the technology
we need to track these weapons when they get

00:28:24.121 --> 00:28:25.121
to another country?

00:28:25.121 --> 00:28:30.520
Or is it that we just don't have the will
because we don't view it as a huge problem?

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:32.919
HARTUNG: I think it's a combination of the
two.

00:28:32.919 --> 00:28:36.730
We definitely need more resources for end
use monitoring, but I think because there's

00:28:36.730 --> 00:28:42.659
a bias towards selling, there's less interest
perhaps in keeping track of how the weapons

00:28:42.659 --> 00:28:44.590
are used in a systematic way.

00:28:44.590 --> 00:28:46.769
So I think the two sort of go together.

00:28:46.769 --> 00:28:51.130
There's a resource issue, and there's an issue
of political strategic priorities that come

00:28:51.130 --> 00:28:52.130
into play.

00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:56.870
SIERRA: Have we ever gone and been like, Okay,
well, you broke our deal, and we are demanding

00:28:56.870 --> 00:28:58.990
these back, or we're gonna go get them back?

00:28:58.990 --> 00:29:01.559
Is that sort of just not on the table, like
as an option?

00:29:01.559 --> 00:29:04.210
STOHL: So we don't usually say, "Can we have
those back?"

00:29:04.210 --> 00:29:06.430
There’s a couple exceptions.

00:29:06.430 --> 00:29:12.230
For example, the United States provided stinger
missiles to Afghanistan in the late 70s, and

00:29:12.230 --> 00:29:16.840
they caused a lot of problems for U.S. forces
even decades later.

00:29:16.840 --> 00:29:22.970
And so in the early 2000s, the United States
actually engaged in a buyback program for

00:29:22.970 --> 00:29:28.750
what's called MANPADS, man-portable air-defense
systems, and has sort of bought those back.

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:29.902
That's a really hard thing to do.

00:29:29.902 --> 00:29:33.789
It's not something the U.S. government engages
in all that regularly.

00:29:33.789 --> 00:29:39.049
That said, we have other tools in our toolbox
to be able to stop sales if they are particularly

00:29:39.049 --> 00:29:40.419
problematic.

00:29:40.419 --> 00:29:46.779
If they are used in ways that are in contravention
with the end user agreement, if they are used

00:29:46.779 --> 00:29:51.670
to commit human rights violations or if they're
used in conflict that were not sort of the

00:29:51.670 --> 00:29:52.990
purpose of that sale.

00:29:52.990 --> 00:29:55.159
So we can stop sales.

00:29:55.159 --> 00:29:57.580
We don't send everything all at once.

00:29:57.580 --> 00:30:00.399
We may not grant future licenses.

00:30:00.399 --> 00:30:07.340
So there can be some sort of punitive measures
that are taken if a government or an end user

00:30:07.340 --> 00:30:08.880
does not act responsibly.

00:30:08.880 --> 00:30:13.909
SIERRA: We wanted to know what it was like
seeing and handling this process on the ground,

00:30:13.909 --> 00:30:19.460
and, as she said before, Christa has had experience
in the field when it comes to oversight.

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:24.250
SIERRA: You did this firsthand, you were on
the ground, you talked about counting serial

00:30:24.250 --> 00:30:25.280
numbers.

00:30:25.280 --> 00:30:29.529
Ultimately this is a business, a lethal business.

00:30:29.529 --> 00:30:34.160
I wonder if you could speak a little bit to
how it's felt for you in this process.

00:30:34.160 --> 00:30:36.710
ALMONTE: You have to see this from both ways.

00:30:36.710 --> 00:30:40.500
There was a book that was written by Andrew
Feinstein called Shadow World.

00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:44.150
I'm guessing by the title, you can probably
guess how he felt about arms trade, but it

00:30:44.150 --> 00:30:46.340
was called Shadow World and the Global Arms
Trade.

00:30:46.340 --> 00:30:50.450
Essentially, it was an exposé, and it was
fantastically written.

00:30:50.450 --> 00:30:55.740
And if you've read it and don't have any experience
in the arms trade, you would say, "My God,

00:30:55.740 --> 00:30:57.640
what are we doing around the world?"

00:30:57.640 --> 00:31:02.899
But you have to look at what I have already
enumerated, and that is the many, many, many

00:31:02.899 --> 00:31:08.950
methods we use to ensure things like what
are covered in that book do not happen today.

00:31:08.950 --> 00:31:12.750
That the arms trade of years ago doesn't happen
today.

00:31:12.750 --> 00:31:14.500
And that's how I tried to look at it.

00:31:14.500 --> 00:31:20.850
When I was in Bahrain, a great ally of ours,
thousands upon thousands of American soldiers

00:31:20.850 --> 00:31:23.630
and sailors passed through there over the
years, including me.

00:31:23.630 --> 00:31:26.410
I've been stationed there three different
times.

00:31:26.410 --> 00:31:28.260
They're in no danger of being overthrown.

00:31:28.260 --> 00:31:30.649
They're never going to turn a weapon on us.

00:31:30.649 --> 00:31:36.210
But I went over as a fresh young officer,
and I saw these operations and I spoke to

00:31:36.210 --> 00:31:41.730
their military, and I literally carried weapons
to be delivered in the back of my vehicle

00:31:41.730 --> 00:31:44.710
to their bases, and I delivered them in person.

00:31:44.710 --> 00:31:47.910
Over the serial numbers they would read off
and I would read off and we would sign the

00:31:47.910 --> 00:31:51.170
bottom saying, "Yep, you now have control
of this weapon."

00:31:51.170 --> 00:31:57.639
It crossed my mind more than once a day, I
would say, that these weapons have been used

00:31:57.639 --> 00:31:59.139
against us in the past.

00:31:59.139 --> 00:32:04.300
Again, my husband and my husband's brother,
a lot of my friends have been deployed into

00:32:04.300 --> 00:32:07.549
Iraq and Afghanistan over the years.

00:32:07.549 --> 00:32:11.549
And it's something you must, all of America
that's involved in this process must keep

00:32:11.549 --> 00:32:13.230
that in the back of their mind.

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:19.760
If we don't, then we will overlook something
in the process and we will sell to the wrong

00:32:19.760 --> 00:32:21.659
person at the wrong time.

00:32:21.659 --> 00:32:26.059
So if we are not questioning ourselves with
each and every sale, we are doing something

00:32:26.059 --> 00:32:28.769
wrong, and that's how I felt about it.

00:32:28.769 --> 00:32:34.470
I read Feinstein's book because, even though
I was in the arms business, I wanted to understand

00:32:34.470 --> 00:32:38.340
where we could go wrong, to avoid going wrong.

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:45.149
SIERRA: I can't help but think about this
in very simple terms.

00:32:45.149 --> 00:32:51.260
If you give a neighbor a gun to defend themselves
or you sell it to them, you can't really control

00:32:51.260 --> 00:32:53.679
how they choose to use it.

00:32:53.679 --> 00:32:58.059
There will always be a risk that one day it
could be used for evil.

00:32:58.059 --> 00:33:02.590
I think that's how a lot of people feel about
the U.S. arms trade, that in the end it will

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:04.870
always fuel death.

00:33:04.870 --> 00:33:09.940
So, what thoughts do you have about the morality
of the arms trade?

00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:15.299
HARTUNG: There's many - too many cases of
arming repressive regimes that are causing

00:33:15.299 --> 00:33:17.250
huge humanitarian suffering.

00:33:17.250 --> 00:33:22.000
And so in that case, when it's being done
knowing that that's going to be the result,

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:23.250
I think it's immoral.

00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:24.510
I think it shouldn't happen.

00:33:24.510 --> 00:33:29.660
You know, if we focused in on the most problematic
sales and made it harder for those to happen,

00:33:29.660 --> 00:33:33.360
the world would be a much better place, the
United States would be safer, many fewer people

00:33:33.360 --> 00:33:34.539
would be suffering.

00:33:34.539 --> 00:33:38.920
And because it's not the whole trade that's
at issue, some of these arguments about the

00:33:38.920 --> 00:33:41.260
economics of it a little bit fall by the wayside.

00:33:41.260 --> 00:33:46.270
I mean, it's not most advocates of arms transfer
control are not saying shut down the whole

00:33:46.270 --> 00:33:47.270
arms trade.

00:33:47.270 --> 00:33:50.280
They're saying, let's not sell to these repressive
regimes.

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:51.919
Let's take a look at how the weapons are being
used.

00:33:51.919 --> 00:33:57.230
All those things can be done and they'll affect
a portion of the trade, but not the entire

00:33:57.230 --> 00:33:58.230
trade.

00:33:58.230 --> 00:34:01.779
So I think some distinctions have to be made,
but there certainly is an element where you

00:34:01.779 --> 00:34:07.529
see, either an immoral or amoral approach
that has devastating human consequences.

00:34:07.529 --> 00:34:12.409
STOHL: I wish I saw the world in a more sort
of black and white way, but I don't actually

00:34:12.409 --> 00:34:17.360
take a principled position on whether arms
transfers are inherently good or inherently

00:34:17.360 --> 00:34:18.360
bad.

00:34:18.360 --> 00:34:19.889
I think they serve a purpose.

00:34:19.889 --> 00:34:26.700
The problem is, and sort of where I am concerned
is they all too often take place sort of irresponsibly

00:34:26.700 --> 00:34:30.290
or unaccountably or without transparency.

00:34:30.290 --> 00:34:32.139
That's where I see the challenge.

00:34:32.139 --> 00:34:37.339
And I think the other challenge for me is
that they're often seen as a tool, a first

00:34:37.339 --> 00:34:41.480
resort, rather than what are our other opportunities?

00:34:41.480 --> 00:34:44.099
Are there diplomatic solutions?

00:34:44.099 --> 00:34:51.040
And if we decide, nope, this is the way to
go, are we putting in place measures to mitigate

00:34:51.040 --> 00:34:56.730
any risks that we have identified in sort
of our deep thinking about this particular

00:34:56.730 --> 00:34:57.730
sale?

00:34:57.730 --> 00:35:02.560
So I see there are some really serious challenges
and problems within the current U.S. security

00:35:02.560 --> 00:35:04.660
cooperation enterprise.

00:35:04.660 --> 00:35:09.510
And those problems are going to continue to
impact the lives of people all over the world.

00:35:09.510 --> 00:35:14.500
In some cases, we've seen blow back from those
weapons against U.S. forces, but also against

00:35:14.500 --> 00:35:15.500
U.S. interests.

00:35:15.500 --> 00:35:20.360
And so I think we have to, again, not just
look at the short term, but think about those

00:35:20.360 --> 00:35:23.820
medium and long term consequences.

00:35:23.820 --> 00:35:28.480
And I think if we are going to continue to
sell weapons for whatever reason, we need

00:35:28.480 --> 00:35:34.490
to do so in an incredibly responsible and
accountable and transparent way.

00:35:34.490 --> 00:35:41.099
And all transfers should really reflect our
morals, our values, our understanding of decades

00:35:41.099 --> 00:35:46.760
of best practice, and in ways that we know
are going to contribute to long term interests

00:35:46.760 --> 00:35:48.060
and outcomes.

00:35:48.060 --> 00:35:50.720
These are not like, "We'll sell them and see
what happens."

00:35:50.720 --> 00:35:56.490
We really need to be thoughtful about how
we utilize arms transfers in our foreign policy

00:35:56.490 --> 00:36:06.870
and national security goals.

